/* The policies people are expected to follow, held in the app so they can be read
   and searched without a connection. */
const POLICY_DOCS = [{"id":"manual","title":"Organisation Policy Manual","short":"Manual","version":"1.0","effective":"1 January 2026","sections":[{"n":"1.1","t":"Mission","b":"To enable survivors of trafficking — particularly bride trafficking — and marginalised communities to lead the cause of non-violence, gender equality, and a feminist movement for social justice in India."},{"n":"1.2","t":"Vision","b":"A world where every individual — especially women, girls, and survivors of trafficking — has the freedom, dignity, and resources to determine their own future, and where communities collectively reject violence, exploitation, and inequality."},{"n":"1.3","t":"History and Context","b":"Empower People was founded in 2006 as a continuation of the Career Development Centre (CDC), which had been working since 1999 to provide education to marginalised youth. The organisation evolved to focus specifically on combating bride trafficking and empowering survivors after encountering a trafficking survivor seeking help. Over more than two decades, the organisation has: Conducted multiple nationwide marches to raise awareness (4,400 km in 2012; 14,000 km in 2018) Identified over 1,000 trafficking cases and supported rehabilitation for survivors Created 23 community-based organisations (CBOs) led entirely by survivors Formed 278 survivor-led communes across multiple states Provided education, skills training, and economic empowerment to over 100,000 women Built a network of survivors trained in journalism, community leadership, and advocacy"},{"n":"1.4","t":"Areas of Operation","b":"Empower People currently operates across Delhi, Haryana, Bihar, and Assam, with programme reach extending to Rajasthan, Gujarat, West Bengal, Jharkhand, and other states through partner and network activities."},{"n":"1.5","t":"Legal and Registration Information","b":"Chapter 2: Core Values and Guiding Principles All policies, programmes, and decisions at Empower People are guided by the following eight core principles."},{"n":"2.1","t":"Human Rights First","b":"All actions must uphold dignity, equality, and justice. Every person, regardless of background, holds inherent rights that the organisation is bound to respect and protect."},{"n":"2.2","t":"Survivor-Centred Approach","b":"Survivors of trafficking are not victims to be rescued, but leaders and change-makers. Safety, consent, agency, and confidentiality of survivors guide all decisions. All programmes and policies prioritise survivor participation, voice, and decision-making."},{"n":"2.3","t":"Zero Tolerance for Harm","b":"Abuse, harassment, sexual exploitation and abuse (SEA), trafficking, coercion, bullying, discrimination, or any misuse of power are strictly prohibited. The organisation adopts a zero-tolerance approach aligned with international PSEA standards."},{"n":"2.4","t":"Transparency and Accountability","b":"Decisions and finances must be ethical, fair, and well-documented. The organisation holds itself accountable to survivors, donors, partners, communities, and the law."},{"n":"2.5","t":"Inclusivity and Equity","b":"No discrimination is permitted on the basis of gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, religion, caste, ethnicity, disability, age, marital status, or socio-economic status. Safeguarding protections apply universally."},{"n":"2.6","t":"Community Empowerment and Collective Action","b":"Communities are agents of their own change, not passive beneficiaries. Solutions must reflect community realities and leadership. Sustainable change emerges from collective action and shared responsibility."},{"n":"2.7","t":"Learning and Reflection","b":"Policies and practices evolve with experience, evaluation, survivor feedback, and organisational learning. Continuous improvement is a standing commitment."},{"n":"2.8","t":"Compliance with Law","b":"All organisational activities must follow the legal framework of India and the requirements of donors, regulators, and international frameworks to which the organisation is accountable. Chapter 3: Governance Structure and Roles"},{"n":"3.1","t":"Governance Framework","b":"Empower People operates under a three-tier integrated governance model that balances strategic oversight, operational decision-making, and field-level implementation. Tier 1 — Executive Committee (Policy and Operations) The Executive Committee oversees implementation of policies, approves programmes and budgets, and handles operational decisions between Board meetings. Current members: Mohd. Shafiqur Rahman Khan (Founder and General Secretary) Shukoh Albadar Khan (Member) Jyoti Goyal (Member) Roohi Sahay (Member) Seema Pawar (Member) Key responsibilities: Approval of annual operational budgets and major programmes Monthly financial and programme monitoring Oversight of staff management and disciplinary matters Approval of expenditures above management delegation limits Risk management and compliance oversight Meeting frequency: Monthly minimum. Quorum: 50% of members (minimum 3 of 5). Decisions by majority vote; the President holds a casting vote. Tier 2 — Board of Advisors (Strategic Input) The Board of Advisors provides strategic guidance, sector expertise, and links to broader networks. Current members: Ms. Charu Raizada Dr. Sabu Mathew George Ms. Mona Dutt Prinjha Tier 3 — Management Team (Day-to-Day Operations) The Management Team oversees day-to-day implementation of policies, management of staff and programmes, and execution of decisions made by the Board and Executive Committee."},{"n":"3.2","t":"Departmental Structure","b":"All operations are divided into three departments, each led by a direct report to the Founder and General Secretary. Department 1 — Finance, Administration and Compliance Director ACF: Bhumish Khudkhudia Functions: Accounts; Procurement and Logistics; Administration and Communication; Compliance. Accountant: Jitendra Thakur Procurement and Logistics: Salauddin (also supervises Support Staff and Drivers) Coordinator – Administration and Communication: Zainab Khan Admin Assistant – Yamunanagar: Minakshi Admin Assistant – Bihar (Sherghati): Mohammad Iqbal Department 2 — Programmes Program Officer: Alishika Raj Functions: Sakhi Mandal; Nari Nusrat; Field Operations; Bihar. Program Coordination Lead: Anjule Shyam Maurya Program Lead – Sakhi Mandal: Rajbala (Paro Voice and Community Media: Shilpa; Azaad Women Enterprise) Program Lead – Nari Nusrat: Noor Yasmin (Ami Paro and Community Media: Akiya Khatoon; Program Coordinator – Coaching Centre: Shah Alam) Coordinator – Field Operations (Haryana): Rajendra Prasad Field Coordinator (Haryana): Dildar Hussain Field Coordinator – M3-SAGE (Bihar): Raushan Kumar Department 3 — Project Development, Research and Innovations Coordinator – Fundraising, Research and Innovation: Zubana Azhar Functions: Fundraising and Partnerships; Research and Innovation; Donor Relations; Communications and Media; Volunteers."},{"n":"3.3","t":"Designated Officers","b":""},{"n":"3.4","t":"Reporting and Communication Flow","b":"Upward reporting: Field → Department → CEO → Executive Committee. Safeguarding escalation: Any staff → Designated Officer (immediate) → CEO (within 4 hours) → External authorities as required. All Department Heads submit monthly reports to the CEO. The CEO submits quarterly reports to the Executive Committee. Chapter 4: Human Resource Policies"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Employment Categories","b":"Empower People engages personnel under the following categories, each with defined contracts, responsibilities, and compensation frameworks: Full-Time Staff Part-Time Staff Consultants and Freelancers Interns Volunteers Community and Survivor Leaders"},{"n":"4.2","t":"Equal Opportunity and Non-Discrimination","b":"Empower People is committed to equal opportunity for all staff, volunteers, and job applicants. No person shall be discriminated against on the basis of gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, race, ethnicity, caste, religion, age, disability, marital or family status, pregnancy or maternity, national or social origin. All decisions on recruitment, promotion, training, and termination are based solely on merit, competence, and organisational need."},{"n":"4.3","t":"Safer Recruitment","b":"All recruitment processes shall be transparent and merit-based, complying with equal opportunity principles. For any role involving contact with children, survivors, or vulnerable persons, the following are mandatory before the person begins work: Police verification / criminal records check Written declaration of any previous criminal history Two character references (known for minimum two years, not family) Signed Child Protection Code of Conduct and Safeguarding commitment Signed agreement with the Organisation Policy Manual Preference in recruitment is given to candidates with lived experience relevant to the organisational mission, including survivors of trafficking, where appropriate. Recruitment follows a two-step process: application screening, followed by interview and reference checks. Absolute exclusion: No person with a known history of abuse, exploitation, violence against women or children, sexual misconduct, or trafficking-related offences shall be engaged in any role that could place people at risk."},{"n":"4.4","t":"Induction","b":"All new staff, volunteers, interns, and consultants must complete a full induction within their first two weeks. Induction covers: organisational mission, values, and programmes; governance structure and reporting lines; all relevant policies; safeguarding and child protection; financial procedures; code of conduct and commitment statement signing."},{"n":"4.5","t":"Code of Conduct","b":"Required Conduct — all personnel must: Every person covered by this manual must: Treat all people with respect, dignity, and fairness Perform duties competently, punctually, and responsibly Maintain professional boundaries at all times Obtain informed consent before collecting stories, images, recordings, or case details Protect privacy and identity, especially where disclosure could increase stigma, retaliation, or danger Use digital tools, social media, and messaging platforms responsibly and safely Declare any actual or potential conflicts of interest to management Report any unsafe, exploitative, or inappropriate conduct immediately Prohibited Conduct — all personnel must not: Every person covered by this manual must not: Engage in sexual activity, sexual contact, suggestive behaviour, or exploitative relationships with beneficiaries, survivors, children, or colleagues Exchange money, aid, services, employment, or protection for sexual favours or personal benefit Use threats, coercion, intimidation, humiliation, or retaliation Hit, shame, isolate, or verbally abuse a child or adult Invite beneficiaries into private or unsafe settings without authorisation and risk assessment Share personally identifiable information, testimonies, or recordings without informed consent Falsify documents, timesheets, or expense claims Engage in fraud, theft, corruption, or bribery Be under the influence of alcohol or illegal substances while working Ignore, minimise, conceal, or delay reporting a safeguarding concern"},{"n":"4.6","t":"Working Hours and Leave","b":"Standard working hours are 9:30 AM to 5:30 PM, Monday to Saturday. Flexibility is permitted in field roles as required. Leave Entitlements"},{"n":"4.7","t":"Performance Management","b":"Performance of all staff is monitored through regular supervision and feedback (minimum monthly), quarterly performance reviews, and annual appraisals. Supervision notes are kept by the supervisor. Annual appraisals are signed by the staff member, their supervisor, and the relevant Department Head."},{"n":"4.8","t":"Grievance and Complaint Procedures","b":"All staff and volunteers have the right to raise grievances or complaints. The process is: Initial complaint to immediate supervisor or management (verbal or written) If unresolved or if the supervisor is the subject of the complaint, escalation to the relevant Department Head or Director ACF If still unresolved, formal escalation to the CEO If the CEO is the subject, escalation to the Chair of the Board Formal investigation and response within 15 days Appeal to the Executive Committee if dissatisfied with the outcome All complaints are handled confidentially with full protection against retaliation. Retaliation against any person who raises a genuine concern is itself serious misconduct."},{"n":"4.9","t":"Whistleblower Protection","b":"Empower People protects those who report genuine concerns about violations of law or policy, unethical conduct, abuse, financial irregularities, or risks to child or survivor safety. Protections include: Confidentiality of identity (unless legally required to disclose) Protection from retaliation, victimisation, or adverse treatment Right to escalate concerns externally if internal processes fail or are unsafe Retaliation against a whistleblower constitutes gross misconduct and will itself be investigated and disciplined. Where internal channels are unsafe or unavailable, staff have the right to report concerns directly to external authorities including police, child protection services, relevant statutory commissions, donor complaint mechanisms, or other legal frameworks applicable in their jurisdiction."},{"n":"4.10","t":"Training and Development","b":"All staff and volunteers shall receive: induction training on organisational mission, values, and policies; role-specific training appropriate to their position; annual refresher training on key policies including safeguarding and child protection; and opportunities for professional development. Attendance at mandatory training is a condition of employment. See Chapter 12 for the full mandatory training programme."},{"n":"4.11","t":"Exit and Termination","b":"Employment may be terminated by the organisation or the employee with notice as per the employment contract and applicable law. Grounds for termination by the organisation include end of project or funding, poor performance despite support, serious or gross misconduct, safeguarding breaches, and conviction for crimes involving abuse, violence, or dishonesty. Notice period: minimum 30 days by either party, unless otherwise specified in the employment contract. An exit interview is conducted for all departing staff. Final dues and certificates are cleared within 45 days of exit. All organisational property must be returned on the last working day. Confidentiality obligations continue after employment ends. All staff sign a Non-Disclosure Agreement upon joining. Chapter 5: Corrective Action Policy"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Purpose and Scope","b":"This policy establishes a fair, transparent, and supportive framework for addressing employee performance issues, policy violations, and misconduct. It applies to all permanent staff, fixed-term and contract staff engaged for one month or more, volunteers and interns engaged for one month or more, and consultants working on organisational projects. Guiding principles: Fairness and impartiality; progressive discipline; support and coaching; transparency; confidentiality; and consistency across the organisation."},{"n":"5.2","t":"Types of Issues","b":"Performance Issues Failure to meet job expectations, targets, or quality standards despite having capability and resources. Examples include consistently missing deadlines, quality of work below acceptable standards, repeated errors, poor attendance, or not responding to feedback. Conduct Violations Category A — Minor: First-time minor policy breach, unprofessional communication, dress code violation, use of phone or social media during work hours, leaving early without permission, minor insubordination. Category B — Moderate: Pattern of minor violations after feedback, significant policy breach, insubordination, conflict with colleagues, minor dishonesty, breach of confidentiality affecting the organisation or a survivor. Category C — Serious Misconduct (grounds for immediate dismissal): Violence, threats, or aggression; sexual harassment or discrimination; child abuse or serious safeguarding breach; fraud, theft, or financial misconduct; gross negligence; substance abuse at work; serious insubordination; conviction of a crime affecting suitability for role; serious breach of confidentiality."},{"n":"5.3","t":"Progressive Corrective Action Framework","b":"When to skip levels and proceed directly to termination: violence or threats; sexual harassment or assault; child abuse or serious safeguarding breach; fraud, theft, or embezzlement; gross negligence causing serious harm; serious violation affecting the safety of the organisation, survivors, or staff."},{"n":"5.4","t":"Level 1: Coaching and Informal Verbal Feedback","b":"Within one to two days of the incident, the supervisor meets privately with the employee. The supervisor describes the specific issue, asks for the employee's perspective, clarifies the expectation, listens to barriers, agrees on correction, and offers support. A brief note is kept in the supervisor's file only. This is informal — the employee does not receive a written warning."},{"n":"5.5","t":"Level 2: Formal Verbal Warning","b":"A meeting is scheduled two to three days in advance with written notice. The meeting covers: the specific issue, the relevant policy or expectation, the employee's opportunity to explain, the clear expectation going forward, and the consequence if the issue continues. A Verbal Warning Form is completed and placed in the personnel file. The employee signs to acknowledge receipt. Follow-up is conducted within two weeks."},{"n":"5.6","t":"Level 3: Written Warning","b":"A preliminary investigation is conducted if needed. Written notice is given two to three days in advance, with the employee's right to bring a support person. The Written Warning document describes the specific issue, references the relevant policy, acknowledges any prior coaching or warnings, states clearly that this is a written warning, and explains consequences. Validity period: 12 months. If no further incidents occur within 12 months, the warning may be removed from the active file. The employee signs to acknowledge receipt and has the right to appeal within seven days."},{"n":"5.7","t":"Level 4A: Performance Improvement Plan (PIP)","b":"A PIP is used for continuing performance issues — not serious misconduct. The PIP document includes: the specific performance gap; root cause analysis; clear expected performance standards; a support plan including training, mentoring, and resources; a timeline of 30 to 60 days; milestones and check-in dates; success criteria; and consequences if improvement is not demonstrated. At the end of the PIP period, outcomes are either: successful (employee moves off the PIP); partial improvement (PIP extended with a new timeline); or no improvement (proceed to Level 5 or 6)."},{"n":"5.8","t":"Level 4B: Suspension Pending Investigation","b":"Used where serious misconduct is alleged and continuing work creates risk. The CEO makes the decision to suspend. The suspension is usually paid. During the suspension, the employee must not attend the office (except for investigation meetings), must not contact colleagues about the matter, and must not access organisational systems. The investigation is conducted within seven days. If allegations are not substantiated, the employee returns with no disciplinary action. If substantiated, a disciplinary hearing is held."},{"n":"5.9","t":"Level 5: Final Warning","b":"Issued after a failed PIP, or for multiple violations after prior corrective actions. The employee is given 30 days to demonstrate sustained improvement. This is the last opportunity before termination. The Final Warning is issued by the CEO in writing, signed by the employee."},{"n":"5.10","t":"Level 6: Termination","b":"Termination follows a disciplinary hearing (where time allows) with the right to representation. The employee receives a written termination letter stating the reason, the last working day, and the final settlement amount. All organisational property must be returned. Confidentiality obligations continue after termination."},{"n":"5.11","t":"Documentation and Record-Keeping","b":"All corrective action documents are stored in the employee's locked personnel file in the Director ACF's office. Only the CEO, the Director ACF, and the employee's direct supervisor may access the file. The employee may request to see their own file at any time."},{"n":"5.12","t":"Appeals","b":"Every employee has the right to appeal any formal corrective action (Levels 2 through 6), excluding informal coaching. The employee submits a written appeal to the Director ACF within seven days of receiving the action. The appeal is reviewed by the CEO and Director ACF. If the appeal has merit, a formal hearing is held within 21 days, chaired by an independent panel (CEO, Director ACF, and one independent person). The panel may uphold, modify, or reverse the original action. The decision is communicated in writing within seven days of the hearing. No retaliation for appealing is permitted."},{"n":"5.13","t":"Roles and Responsibilities","b":"Chapter 6: Safeguarding Framework Safeguarding is a mandatory responsibility of every person working with or for Empower People. Failure to report a concern, suspicion, disclosure, or incident may itself constitute a policy breach and may result in disciplinary action."},{"n":"6.1","t":"Scope and Zero-Tolerance Commitments","b":"This framework applies to all physical premises and field locations, online and hybrid events, media interactions involving survivors or children, organisational recruitment and HR processes, and conduct inside and outside working hours where a person's actions may affect the safety, dignity, or reputation of beneficiaries or the organisation. Zero tolerance applies to: sexual exploitation and abuse; child abuse and neglect; trafficking, forced marriage, and coercion; bullying, humiliation, and retaliation; misuse of personal data, images, or testimonies; and abuse of authority or conflict of interest."},{"n":"6.2","t":"Key Definitions","b":""},{"n":"6.3","t":"Guiding Principles","b":"Do No Harm — no programme, communication, or decision should expose a person to additional risk Survivor-Centred Practice — the voice, choice, safety, and dignity of survivors must guide decisions affecting them Best Interests of the Child — in all matters involving children, the child's safety and best interests are paramount Informed Consent — participation, interviews, photography, data collection, referrals, and storytelling must be voluntary and based on clear, understandable information Confidentiality — information is shared strictly on a need-to-know basis and stored securely Non-Discrimination — safeguarding protections apply regardless of gender, caste, religion, age, disability, sexuality, marital status, ethnicity, or migration status Accountability — every concern must be recorded, assessed, and acted upon appropriately No Retaliation — anyone reporting in good faith must be protected from victimisation or adverse consequences Trauma-Informed Practice — staff must recognise trauma impacts and avoid blame, pressure, sensationalism, and re-traumatisation"},{"n":"6.4","t":"Child Protection","b":"Forms of Child Abuse As defined by WHO and national standards: physical abuse; sexual abuse or exploitation; emotional or psychological abuse; and neglect. A child may experience multiple types simultaneously. Code of Conduct for Work with Children Adults must: maintain appropriate boundaries; work in open, visible spaces; report concerns immediately; treat all children with respect and non-discrimination. Adults must not: hit or physically punish children; humiliate, threaten, or degrade children; engage in sexual behaviour or communication; be alone with a child behind closed doors; photograph children without explicit consent. Child Protection Reporting Protocol Step 1: Ensure immediate safety of the child Step 2: Record facts only (no interpretation or conclusions) Step 3: Report verbally to the Child Protection Officer within 2 hours; submit written incident form within 24 hours Step 4: Child Protection Officer conducts internal assessment and notifies the CEO within 4 hours Step 5: External reporting to Childline (1098), CWC, or police as appropriate Step 6: Case closure with full documentation and organisational learning Child Protection Officer: Zubana Azhar — post@empowerpeople.in"},{"n":"6.5","t":"Sexual Harassment (POSH) Policy","b":"Empower People maintains zero tolerance for sexual harassment in the workplace and in all organisational activities, in full compliance with the Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act, 2013. Definition Sexual harassment includes any unwelcome behaviour of a sexual nature, including but not limited to: Physical conduct: Unwelcome physical contact — patting, pinching, stroking, kissing, hugging, fondling, or inappropriate touching Physical violence, including sexual assault Use of job-related threats or rewards to solicit sexual favours Verbal conduct: Comments on a worker’s appearance, age, or private life Sexual comments, stories, and jokes Sexual advances; repeated and unwanted invitations for dates or physical intimacy Insults based on the sex of the worker; condescending or paternalistic remarks Sending sexually explicit messages by phone or email Non-verbal conduct: Display of sexually explicit or suggestive material Sexually suggestive gestures, whistling, or leering Internal Committee (IC) The Internal Committee is constituted as required by law. Composition: a Presiding Officer (senior woman employee); two staff members committed to the cause of women; one external member from an NGO or association committed to the cause of women. At least 50% of members must be women. Complaint Process Anyone experiencing sexual harassment should, if possible, inform the alleged harasser that the conduct is unwanted. If they cannot directly approach the harasser, they may approach the Designated Safeguarding Lead or another designated staff member. On receiving a complaint, the designated person will: Immediately record the dates, times, and facts of the incident(s) Ascertain the views of the complainant as to the desired outcome Ensure the complainant understands the organisation's procedures Discuss and agree next steps — informal or formal — on the understanding that choosing the informal route does not preclude a formal complaint if the outcome is unsatisfactory Keep a confidential record of all discussions Ensure the complainant knows they may also lodge a complaint through the relevant legal framework outside the organisation Informal mechanism: The designated person facilitates discussion between both parties to achieve an acceptable informal resolution, or refers the matter to a designated mediator. The process is completed within seven days of the complaint being made. Formal mechanism: If the informal mechanism does not resolve the matter, or if the complainant chooses the formal route, a written complaint is submitted to the Internal Committee. The IC inquiry begins within seven days; the IC report is completed within ten days of the conclusion of the inquiry. The IC submits an annual report on cases received, disposed of, and pending, along with findings and recommendations. IC Annual Calendar The IC meets once every quarter as per prior notification by the Presiding Officer Minutes of meetings are prepared by a committee member and shared with the CEO within 10 days of the meeting The IC prepares a calendar and annual report each year and submits it to the CEO Monitoring and Evaluation Empower People anonymously collects statistics and data on how the POSH policy is used and whether it is effective. Supervisors and those handling cases report annually on compliance, including number of incidents, how they were dealt with, and recommendations made. It is the responsibility of every manager to ensure all staff in their team are aware of this policy. Protections No retaliation against a complainant, witness, or support person Transfer or leave options available to the complainant during inquiry Full confidentiality of all proceedings"},{"n":"6.6","t":"Prevention of Sexual Exploitation and Abuse (PSEA)","b":"Sexual exploitation and abuse (SEA) involves the abuse of a position of power, trust, or vulnerability for sexual purposes. It includes any sexual activity with a beneficiary or survivor, even if framed as consensual, because of the inherent power imbalance. Empower People adopts the UN and IASC standards on PSEA. Core commitments: zero tolerance; no sexual relationships with beneficiaries or survivors; mandatory reporting of all suspicions; full accountability of all staff regardless of seniority. Prevention measures include background checks, supervision, regular training, and a confidential reporting channel."},{"n":"6.7","t":"Safeguarding Survivors in Media Interactions","b":"Any media engagement involving survivors must prioritise safety, dignity, informed participation, and control over personal narratives. Survivors are informed of this policy before participating and given a clear process to report any concern or incident of harm during media interactions. Media Oversight Committee A Media Oversight Committee is formed for every media engagement. Composition: three members, of whom at least two must be women; at least two must be from the Executive Committee; one may be from programme staff. All three members must be present for quorum. The committee vets and approves every aspect of the media interaction — the plan, objective, method, team, end output, and all research tools. Any inadequacies must be corrected before the interaction proceeds. The committee has final authority on all matters relating to the media engagement. Data Protection Agreement For any engagement involving an external media team, a Data Protection and Safety Agreement must be prepared and signed by the lead person of the external team before any documents are shared or any field visit begins. The signed copy must be submitted to Empower People's head office at least one week prior to the visit. Permission will not be granted otherwise. Steps for Media Field Visits For Empower People's own staff: Reintroduce the organisation and purpose to all persons present State the purpose in adequate detail Obtain explicit permission for photographs and videos Change the names of survivors and relevant persons Blur the faces of survivors and other relevant persons Ensure all team members are trained on sensitivity requirements For external media teams: Introduce the organisation, company, or institution and each team member State the purpose in adequate detail Obtain explicit permission for photographs and videos Change the names of survivors and relevant persons Blur the faces of survivors and other relevant persons Vetting by the Media Oversight Committee of all questions and interactions Ensure data protection agreement is in place and followed Ensure the safety and security of survivors and related persons throughout Safeguarding Requirements Documented informed consent in a language the person understands, before any media engagement Freedom to refuse, pause, or withdraw at any stage without penalty Options for anonymity, pseudonym, image blurring, or voice masking where any risk exists No disclosure of location, trafficking history, legal case details, or family identity that creates danger No sensational, invasive, or exploitative questioning by media partners Briefing of all media partners on safeguarding expectations before any interaction with survivors or children"},{"n":"6.8","t":"Safeguarding in Online Events and Digital Spaces","b":"Online participation by survivors, children, or vulnerable persons is managed as a safeguarding activity with the same rigour as in-person engagement. For all online events, the organisation shall: Share the purpose, agenda, risks, and participation expectations with all participants in advance Identify a safeguarding focal person for the event and share their contact details with participants Establish ground rules for respectful communication and confidentiality at the start of every session Monitor chat, comments, and participant behaviour for harmful conduct throughout the event Avoid recording sessions involving sensitive content without explicit consent from all participants Offer opt-out options if discussion becomes distressing or triggering for any participant Review post-event concerns and provide follow-up support where needed Provide participants with a feedback form after the event to report any concerns or feedback on the safeguarding approach Review all feedback received and take necessary action to improve the policy and prevent future incidents"},{"n":"6.9","t":"Safeguarding Reporting Duties and Timeframes","b":"Any staff member, volunteer, consultant, board member, or representative who witnesses, suspects, receives a disclosure of, or is informed about a safeguarding concern must report it immediately. Reporting channels: Child-related concerns → Child Protection Officer (Zubana Azhar). Adult safeguarding, harassment, misconduct, or survivor-related concerns → Designated Safeguarding Lead (Jyoti Goyal). If designated officers or senior management are implicated → Chair of the Board / Executive Committee. Individuals always have the right to report directly to external authorities (police, child protection services, statutory commissions, or relevant donor complaint mechanisms)."},{"n":"6.10","t":"Response Procedure","b":"On receiving a safeguarding concern, the organisation follows a structured, survivor-centred response: listen calmly; ensure immediate safety; assess risk; notify the designated officer; record the concern; initiate a response plan; investigate impartially; support the affected person; and close the case with documentation and organisational learning."},{"n":"6.11","t":"Investigation Standards","b":"All safeguarding investigations must be timely, impartial, confidential, survivor-sensitive, and fair to all parties. Where risk is credible, the organisation shall consider temporary suspension or removal from duties of the alleged perpetrator, and shall ensure no contact between the alleged perpetrator and the affected person during assessment. An investigation panel with no conflict of interest shall be appointed. Full written records of all steps, evidence, decisions, and actions are maintained."},{"n":"6.12","t":"Support for Affected Persons","b":"Any person affected by a safeguarding incident shall be offered appropriate, timely, and non-judgmental support: immediate safety planning; medical care and emergency assistance; trauma-informed emotional support or counselling; legal information and referral; safe transport or accompaniment; and adjustments to programme participation, duties, or communication channels. The organisation shall not pressure survivors or complainants to mediate, reconcile, withdraw, or remain silent."},{"n":"6.13","t":"Safeguarding Contacts","b":"Chapter 7: Financial Management and Procedures"},{"n":"7.1","t":"Financial Principles","b":"All financial management at Empower People is guided by: accountability (clear tracking of all income and expenditure); transparency (disclosure of financial information to stakeholders); integrity (ethical and honest financial conduct); efficiency (responsible use of resources to maximise impact); and compliance (adherence to legal and donor requirements)."},{"n":"7.2","t":"Payment Authorisation Framework","b":"The following approval structure governs all payments and expenditures: Key principles: The person requesting a payment is never the approver or payer. Department heads only verify — the Director ACF approves everything. The Director ACF's own requests go directly to the Founder and General Secretary regardless of amount. Payments above ₹50,000 come to the Founder and General Secretary for final approval and bank authorisation. Three Payment Flows Salary: Zainab Khan (Administration and Communication) prepares the salary input → straight to Finance (no separate functional verification stage) Programme and Activity: Field team raises request → Anjule Shyam Maurya or Alishika Raj verifies → Director ACF approves → Finance Procurement: Programme or admin team raises the requirement → Salauddin handles quotations and vendor process (he does not verify) → Director ACF verifies and approves → Finance"},{"n":"7.3","t":"Voucher Documentation Requirements","b":"Every payment voucher must include: bill number; cheque number or transaction number; and complete detailed narration. Vouchers must be accompanied by: original bills; photocopies of cheques; UPI receipts where applicable; activity reports; photographs (minimum four per activity); and participant or beneficiary lists with signatures. All vouchers are verified with signatures by both the Accountant and the Founder and General Secretary. Expenses of the Founder and General Secretary are approved by the Treasurer. Only genuine printed bills are accepted. Handwritten cash memos are not accepted. All bills must be in the name of Empower People, not in the name of individuals."},{"n":"7.4","t":"Ordering and Procurement","b":"Written purchase orders are required for all orders above ₹1,000. A minimum of three written quotations are required for purchases of ₹2,000 or more from a single vendor, and for all purchases above ₹10,000. All procurement records must be maintained and available for audit."},{"n":"7.5","t":"Petty Cash","b":"The petty cash system operates on an imprest basis. Maximum per transaction: ₹2,000. Maximum float: ₹10,000. Reconciliation must be completed monthly or whenever the float is restored. All transactions must be documented with receipts. Petty cash for domestic and FCRA purposes is under the joint custody of the Accountant and the Founder and General Secretary. Prepaid cards and UPI payments are treated as cash equivalents and must follow the same security protocols. Petty cash from different sources must not be mixed. Any cash income is banked through the Director ACF and must not be used for petty cash expenditure. Cash received in-house is banked weekly; cash from out-of-house events is banked immediately after the event. Cash is kept in locked metal cabinets at all times."},{"n":"7.6","t":"Advances","b":"Advances must be requested in writing with estimated expenses, and approved by the relevant Department Head or Director ACF. Settlement is required within 30 days of project completion, supported by original receipts. Unsettled balances may be deducted from salary."},{"n":"7.7","t":"Cheque and Payment Authorisation","b":"Signatories do not sign cheques payable to themselves. No blank cheques are permitted."},{"n":"7.8","t":"Bank Accounts","b":"FCRA and domestic accounts are maintained and audited separately. A local branch of any cooperative bank may be opened with written approval of the Executive Committee. All income is deposited as soon as possible, no less than once a week. The FCRA accounts are subject to separate audit as required under FCRA regulations."},{"n":"7.9","t":"Salaries and Payroll","b":"Monthly salaries are paid on the 5th of the following month (or the nearest working day) by direct bank transfer. A payslip is issued with all deductions. All statutory obligations (income tax, PF, PAYE) are met on schedule. Salary scales and positions are approved by the Executive Committee and reviewed annually in March for implementation from 1 April. Travelling Allowance"},{"n":"7.10","t":"Income and Donations","b":"Receipt vouchers are issued for all donations. All donation details (donor name, amount, date, purpose) are documented. Funds are banked promptly and recorded in the accounting system within three days. All grant conditions are reviewed, documented, and communicated to the Finance team. Fund usage is tracked separately where required by the donor."},{"n":"7.11","t":"Financial Records and Reporting","b":"Proper accounting records are maintained in Tally and Excel, with manual or paper records used where appropriate. Monthly bank and petty cash reconciliations are completed by the Accountant. A Fixed Assets Register records all assets costing ₹5,000 or above, with details of date of purchase, supplier, cost, serial number, and description. Financial year: 1 April to 31 March. Additional accounting requirements: head and subhead accounts are opened in Tally for each programme activity; a manual ledger is maintained project-wise; two consumable stock registers are maintained (one for stationery, one for groceries); TDS certificates are applied for in May each year; all expenses are processed through office notes; income and expenditure information is recorded within three days; all voucher corrections and adjustments are noted in a written journal with reasons and supporting documentation. No more than 80% of the allocated budget for any programme may be approved without review; expenses may exceed the approved amount by no more than 10% before further approval is required. The Accountant is personally responsible for resolving account and bill discrepancies."},{"n":"7.12","t":"External Audit and Compliance","b":"An independent external audit is conducted annually. Audited accounts are submitted by June each year. The organisation complies with all requirements under the Societies Registration Act, Income Tax Act (80G and 12A), the Foreign Contribution Regulation Act (FCRA), and applicable labour laws."},{"n":"7.13","t":"Financial Integrity and Anti-Corruption","b":"All staff must handle organisational resources ethically, declare conflicts of interest, not accept bribes or corrupt benefits, report financial irregularities, and cooperate with audits and investigations. Financial misconduct may result in investigation, termination, recovery of misappropriated funds, and referral to police or relevant authorities. See also Chapter 9. Chapter 8: Procurement Policy"},{"n":"8.1","t":"Principles","b":"All procurement at Empower People is guided by: transparency; value for money; fairness; accountability; and ethical compliance. Procurement processes must be documented and available for audit at all times."},{"n":"8.2","t":"Procurement Methods","b":""},{"n":"8.3","t":"Supplier Selection","b":"Suppliers are selected on the basis of: cost-effectiveness; quality; delivery timeline; ethical compliance; and safeguarding record (for suppliers who may interact with beneficiaries). No supplier shall be selected on the basis of personal relationships without written justification and independent verification of value."},{"n":"8.4","t":"Conflict of Interest in Procurement","b":"Any staff member with a personal, family, or financial interest in a supplier must declare this in writing before any procurement decision involving that supplier. They must recuse themselves from the selection process. Non-declaration of a conflict of interest is treated as financial misconduct."},{"n":"8.5","t":"Contract Management","b":"Written contracts are required for all supplier relationships above ₹10,000. Contracts must include the scope of work, payment schedule, quality standards, and termination clauses. Performance against the contract must be monitored and documented. Safeguarding clauses are included in all contracts with suppliers who may interact with beneficiaries, survivors, or children."},{"n":"8.6","t":"Record-Keeping","b":"All procurement records (quotations, purchase orders, invoices, contracts, receipts, and correspondence) must be retained for a minimum of seven years and made available for audit on request. Chapter 9: Ethics, Anti-Fraud and Anti-Corruption Policy"},{"n":"9.1","t":"Integrity Requirements","b":"All persons working with Empower People must: act honestly and ethically in all dealings; maintain accurate and complete records; avoid and declare conflicts of interest; protect confidential information; comply with all applicable laws and donor requirements; and not engage in fraud, bribery, corruption, or any form of financial misconduct."},{"n":"9.2","t":"Zero Tolerance for Fraud and Corruption","b":"Fraud, bribery, and corruption are strictly prohibited. This includes: misappropriation of funds; falsification of records, receipts, or timesheets; acceptance of bribes or corrupt benefits; conflict of interest that advantages a staff member financially; and misuse of organisational assets."},{"n":"9.3","t":"Respect for Human Rights","b":"Empower People respects and upholds the fundamental human rights and dignity of all individuals, regardless of race, gender, ethnicity, religion, sexual orientation, disability, or socio-economic status. The organisation is committed to promoting gender equality, inclusivity, and non-discrimination in all aspects of its work."},{"n":"9.4","t":"Transparency and Openness","b":"Empower People operates with complete transparency in its finances, operations, and decision-making. The organisation publishes: an annual report covering programmes and outcomes; audited financial statements; and its governance structure. All stakeholders may request a copy of these documents. The organisation maintains open channels of communication with stakeholders, encouraging feedback, participation, and collaboration in all activities."},{"n":"9.5","t":"Conflict of Interest","b":"A conflict of interest arises when a person's personal, family, or financial interests compete with the interests of the organisation. All conflicts of interest — actual or potential — must be declared in writing to the relevant Department Head or the CEO. The person must recuse themselves from any decision in which they have a conflict. Senior staff and board members are required to submit an annual declaration of interests."},{"n":"9.6","t":"Detection and Reporting","b":"Financial irregularities are detected through: regular internal audits; random management checks on bank and cash; finance committee oversight; reconciliation and variance analysis; and anonymous whistleblower reporting. Any staff member who suspects fraud, corruption, or financial misconduct must report it immediately to the CEO or, if the CEO is implicated, to the Chair of the Board. Whistleblower protections apply fully (see Section 4.7)."},{"n":"9.7","t":"Response to Financial Misconduct","b":"On receipt of a credible report of financial misconduct: the CEO is notified immediately; the alleged perpetrator may be suspended pending investigation; an investigation panel is appointed; findings are documented; and if substantiated, the matter may result in termination, recovery of funds, and referral to police or other authorities. Chapter 10: Operations and Programmes"},{"n":"10.1","t":"Programme Principles","b":"All programmes are designed and implemented according to: mission alignment; evidence-based approaches; participatory design with survivors and communities; clear outcome objectives; sustainability through local leadership; and high standards of quality and safeguarding."},{"n":"10.2","t":"Key Programmatic Areas","b":""},{"n":"10.3","t":"Community-Based Organisations and Communes","b":"Empower People supports 23 survivor-led CBOs and 278 survivor-led communes across its areas of operation. These structures are led entirely by trafficking survivors. Each CBO has a President or Chairperson, Secretary, Treasurer, and General Members. CBOs operate independently with organisational support and are responsible for their own activities, local advocacy, and monthly reporting to Empower People."},{"n":"10.4","t":"Risk Assessment and Safe Programming","b":"All programmes, meetings, travel, training sessions, media activities, and digital engagements must assess foreseeable safeguarding risks before implementation. Risk assessment must consider: participant profile (age, gender, disability, language, and trauma); power dynamics between staff and participants; privacy and confidentiality risks; venue safety, transport, accommodation, and supervision; digital risks (recording, doxxing, impersonation, unauthorised access); and community risks (stigma, retaliation, family violence). Where risks are identified, the Programme Officer must put in place specific mitigation measures including informed consent procedures, separate support contacts, restricted access, moderation, transport planning, and referral options."},{"n":"10.5","t":"Survivor Leadership and Agency","b":"Survivors have a meaningful voice in the design and implementation of all programmes that affect them. Decision-making processes include survivor representation and consultation. Survivors are supported to lead, not merely to participate. Their expertise and lived experience are treated as essential knowledge. Survivors are compensated for media work and paid participation activities."},{"n":"10.6","t":"Monitoring and Evaluation","b":"All programmes are monitored and evaluated to track progress toward objectives, measure impact and outcomes, identify challenges and needed adjustments, and demonstrate accountability to stakeholders. Internal assessment mechanisms: Regular data collection: quantitative and qualitative data on programme activities, reach, training, and services Monthly progress reports submitted by all staff Case studies of survivors and at-risk individuals documented to track progress and measure impact Regular staff feedback sessions to gather insights on strategy effectiveness Periodic review meetings with programme stakeholders to assess progress and adjust strategy External assessment mechanisms: Baseline survey at the start of each project to establish key indicators and benchmarks Mid-term evaluation by independent external evaluators, assessing performance against objectives End-line evaluation at project conclusion, using quantitative and qualitative methods Stakeholder interviews with beneficiaries, partner organisations, and community leaders Independent financial audits to ensure transparency and compliance See Chapter 12 for the full reporting framework."},{"n":"10.7","t":"Community and Government Partnerships","b":"Empower People works in partnership with CBOs and communes through MoUs that include safeguarding clauses, regular coordination, and joint planning. The organisation engages with government agencies (ICPS, DCPU, police, district administration) on anti-trafficking efforts, child protection, and survivor support. All partnership agreements include safeguarding compliance as a mandatory condition. Chapter 11: Grant-Making and Partner Selection"},{"n":"11.1","t":"Grant-Making Principles","b":"When Empower People makes grants to other organisations or CBOs, grants are guided by: transparency; accountability; equity; impact; and sustainability. All grant decisions are documented and approved by the Executive Committee."},{"n":"11.2","t":"Grant Cycle","b":"Step 1: Call for proposals — published on the website and through partner networks Step 2: Application submission — via email or online portal Step 3: Screening — eligibility assessment against stated criteria Step 4: Evaluation — committee review of relevance, feasibility, and sustainability Step 5: Decision — Executive Committee approves the final selection Step 6: Agreement — detailed MoU with scope, budget, timelines, and safeguarding clauses Step 7: Monitoring — periodic reports, field visits, and audits Step 8: Closure — final report and financial reconciliation"},{"n":"11.3","t":"Partner Selection Policy","b":"Partners are identified and selected based on: mission alignment; strong reputation and track record; financial stability; capacity to deliver; geographic relevance; and safeguarding compliance. Due diligence steps before formalising any partnership: legal registration check; financial management review; safeguarding policy review; reference checks; and site visit where feasible. All formal partnerships are documented in a signed MoU or agreement that includes mandatory safeguarding clauses, reporting obligations, and the right of Empower People to suspend or terminate the partnership in case of a safeguarding breach. Chapter 12: Staff Reporting and Mandatory Training"},{"n":"12.1","t":"Reporting as Accountability and Learning","b":"Reporting at Empower People is not punitive — it is a mechanism for transparency, accountability, performance monitoring, financial integrity, safeguarding, and continuous learning. Every person has a responsibility to report accurately and on time."},{"n":"12.2","t":"Reporting Cycles","b":"Operational Reports Financial Reports Safeguarding Reports"},{"n":"12.3","t":"Incident Reporting Protocol","b":"Within 2 hours: Verbal or WhatsApp notification to the designated officer Within 24 hours: Written incident form submitted to the designated officer Within 4 hours of notification (for serious incidents): Designated officer notifies CEO Within 7 days (if an allegation): Designated officer initiates investigation; alleged perpetrator suspended if risk is credible Ongoing: Case closure communicated; support provided; learning documented"},{"n":"12.4","t":"Mandatory Training Programme","b":"Tier 1 — Core Training: All Staff Tier 2 — Role-Specific Training In addition to Tier 1, the following role-specific modules are mandatory: Tier 3 — Leadership Training: Department Heads and Senior Staff An additional 12 days per year covering organisational leadership, supervision and performance management, safeguarding leadership, strategic planning, and donor relations."},{"n":"12.5","t":"Training Attendance and Certification","b":"All staff must attend 100% of mandatory training sessions. Excused absences (medical emergency or family crisis) must be documented with supervisor approval, and a make-up session arranged. Training is not charged against annual leave. A signed certificate of participation is issued on completion, with copies held by the staff member, in the personnel file, and with the training coordinator."},{"n":"12.6","t":"Training Evaluation","b":"An evaluation form is completed at the end of each training session. Three months after each training, the supervisor documents whether the staff member has applied the learning in their work. An annual training review is conducted in December–January by the CEO and Director ACF. Chapter 13: Communications and Transparency"},{"n":"13.1","t":"Transparency Principles","b":"Empower People is committed to transparent communication with survivors, donors, partners, government agencies, staff, and the public. Information shared publicly includes: organisational mission, programmes, and values; annual audited financial statements; impact and learning; governance structure; and policies and procedures (on request)."},{"n":"13.2","t":"Annual Reporting","b":"The organisation publishes an Annual Report covering: programme activities and beneficiary numbers; key outcomes and impact; organisational highlights; and audited financial statements. The report is available to all stakeholders on request and on the organisational website."},{"n":"13.3","t":"Media Communications","b":"All media enquiries are directed to the Founder and General Secretary or the Coordinator — Fundraising, Research and Innovation. Only authorised spokespersons speak to the media. Survivor stories are shared only with informed consent and full safeguards in place. The privacy and dignity of survivors are protected at all times. All media engagement involving survivors follows the standards in Section 6.7."},{"n":"13.4","t":"Digital and Social Media","b":"Empower People uses digital platforms (website, social media, Paro Voice, Ami Paro) for awareness-raising and community engagement. All content reflects organisational values and the dignity of survivors. Images and stories are used only with informed consent. All staff are responsible for responsible and safe use of digital tools in their work."},{"n":"13.5","t":"Feedback and Complaints","b":"Stakeholders — including survivors, community members, partners, and donors — may provide feedback or lodge complaints: through programme staff or supervisors; through feedback forms (anonymous if preferred); directly to the CEO or the Executive Committee. All feedback is taken seriously, responded to within 15 days, handled confidentially, and used for learning and improvement. Chapter 14: Data Protection and Confidentiality"},{"n":"14.1","t":"Principles","b":"All personal and case-related information is collected only for legitimate purposes, kept secure, and shared strictly on a need-to-know basis. The organisation complies with all applicable Indian data protection laws."},{"n":"14.2","t":"Organisational Obligations","b":"Use locked storage, password protection, and restricted access for sensitive records Keep incident records separate from general personnel or programme files Seek informed consent before collecting, storing, sharing, or publishing personal information Avoid disclosing identities in reports, training examples, meetings, and public materials unless explicitly authorised and safe Take special precautions with information related to children, trafficking history, sexual violence, health, legal cases, and survivor location"},{"n":"14.3","t":"Informed Consent","b":"Participation, interviews, photography, data collection, referrals, and storytelling must be voluntary and based on clear, understandable information provided in the person's own language. Survivors must be informed of how their information will be used, who will have access, and their right to withdraw consent at any time without penalty."},{"n":"14.4","t":"Mandatory Reporting Without Consent","b":"Mandatory reporting without consent may be required in: cases involving children where national child protection laws impose a duty to report; situations of serious and imminent risk of harm to the survivor or another identifiable person; and safeguarding incidents where national law, regulatory frameworks, or donor rules impose a duty to report externally. In all such cases, staff must explain to the person — as far as safely possible — why mandatory reporting is required and what steps will be taken."},{"n":"14.5","t":"Photography and Video","b":"No photographs or video of survivors, children, or community members shall be taken or used without explicit informed consent. Subjects must understand how the material will be used. Children's faces must be protected and they must never be made identifiable. Consent may be withdrawn at any time. Storage and access are strictly limited."},{"n":"14.6","t":"Breaches of Confidentiality","b":"Breaches of confidentiality — including sharing survivor identities without consent, leaking case details, or misusing personal data — are treated as serious misconduct and may result in disciplinary action up to and including termination, and referral to relevant authorities. Chapter 15: Compliance and Accountability"},{"n":"15.1","t":"Legal Compliance","b":"Empower People complies with all applicable laws and regulations including: Societies Registration Act (SRA), 1860 Income Tax Act — 80G and 12A registration Foreign Contribution Regulation Act (FCRA) Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA) Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act, 2013 Industrial Disputes Act, 1947 Industrial Employment (Standing Orders) Act, 1946 Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972 Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act Applicable data protection and privacy laws"},{"n":"15.2","t":"Policy Compliance","b":"All staff, volunteers, and representatives must: read and understand applicable policies; sign commitment statements at induction; comply fully in all aspects of their work; participate in training and updates; and report violations or concerns immediately. Non-compliance is treated as misconduct and subject to disciplinary action."},{"n":"15.3","t":"Monitoring and Audit","b":"Internal monitoring: monthly progress and financial reports; quarterly Executive Committee reviews; annual organisational performance evaluation; regular supervision and feedback to staff. External monitoring: annual independent financial audit; monitoring visits by donors; periodic programme evaluation by independent evaluators; and compliance checks by government agencies."},{"n":"15.4","t":"Risk Management","b":"The organisation identifies and manages risks in four categories: financial (fraud, misappropriation, funding constraints); safeguarding (child abuse, sexual misconduct, re-exploitation within programmes); operational (staff capacity, programme implementation quality); and reputational (misconduct, financial irregularities, programme failures, external events). Risks are documented, mitigation strategies developed, responsible persons assigned, and risks reviewed quarterly."},{"n":"15.5","t":"Breach of Policy","b":"Chapter 16: Policy Review and Amendment"},{"n":"16.1","t":"Review Cycle","b":"This Organisation Policy Manual shall be reviewed at least annually by the Management Team and Executive Committee, and more frequently if required due to: a serious incident or pattern of incidents; changes in law or donor requirements; new programme models, technology use, or geographic expansion; or significant organisational learning and survivor feedback."},{"n":"16.2","t":"Comprehensive Review","b":""},{"n":"16.3","t":"Amendment Authority","b":"Minor updates (corrections, contact details, staff names): approved by the Management Team and the CEO. Policy changes: approved by the Executive Committee. Major structural changes: approved by the Executive Committee. All revisions are communicated clearly to all personnel."},{"n":"16.4","t":"Emergency Revisions","b":"Emergency revisions are permitted where required for immediate legal or donor compliance. Such revisions are made by the CEO with Executive Committee notification and are ratified at the next scheduled meeting."},{"n":"16.5","t":"Version Control","b":"Each revision receives a new version number and effective date. Superseded versions are archived with their effective period noted. Staff are notified of all revisions within 14 days. Chapter 17: Commitment Statement and Contacts"},{"n":"17.1","t":"Commitment Statement","b":"All board members, staff, volunteers, interns, consultants, and partners must sign a commitment statement acknowledging: I have read and understood this Organisation Policy Manual in full I agree to comply with its standards and procedures at all times I understand my mandatory duty to report safeguarding concerns immediately without delay I understand that breach of this policy may lead to disciplinary action up to termination and referral to external authorities I commit to protecting the safety, dignity, and rights of all survivors, children, and vulnerable persons with whom I work Name (Print): ___________________________________________________ Signature: ______________________________________________________ Date: _________________ Position / Role: ________________________________________________ Department: _____________________________________________________ Witnessed by: ___________________________________________________ Date: _________________"},{"n":"17.2","t":"Organisational Contacts","b":""},{"n":"17.3","t":"Emergency Contacts","b":"This Organisation Policy Manual is effective from 1 January 2026. Version 1.0. Next review due: 1 January 2027. EMPOWER PEOPLE  |  Pocket A-131, Sarita Vihar, New Delhi – 110076 www.empowerpeople.in  |  post@empowerpeople.in"}]},{"id":"hr","title":"HR Policy","short":"HR","version":"current","effective":"reviewed every two years","sections":[{"n":"1","t":"Introduction","b":"Empower People is committed to creating a safe, inclusive, and empowering work environment for all employees, volunteers, interns, and associates. This Human Resource Policy outlines the rights, responsibilities, and expectations of all individuals working with the organisation."},{"n":"2","t":"Vision and Values Alignment","b":"All HR policies are grounded in our organisational values: Dignity and respect for all individuals. A survivor-centric approach in all programmes and operations. Gender sensitivity and inclusion in language, behaviour and policy. Transparency, accountability and ownership at every level."},{"n":"3.1","t":"Types of Engagement","b":"Six categories, each with defined contracts, responsibilities and compensation frameworks: full-time staff; part-time staff; consultants; interns; volunteers; community leaders (Sakhi leaders)."},{"n":"4","t":"Recruitment and Selection","b":"All recruitment will be conducted in a transparent, fair and inclusive manner. Equal opportunity employer: no discrimination based on gender, caste, religion, age, sexual orientation, disability or background. Preference is given to candidates with lived experience related to our mission, for example survivors of trafficking. A two-step process is followed: Step 1, application screening. Step 2, interview and reference checks."},{"n":"5.1","t":"Code of Conduct — expected behaviour","b":"Professionalism and ethical conduct. Respectful language and tone, especially when interacting with Sakhis and marginalised communities. Cultural sensitivity and confidentiality."},{"n":"5.2","t":"Code of Conduct — unacceptable behaviour","b":"Harassment, bullying, or any form of violence. Exploitation of power or position. Discrimination in any form. Violation of this policy will result in disciplinary action, including termination or legal proceedings."},{"n":"6","t":"Anti-Sexual Harassment Policy","b":"Empower People has a zero-tolerance policy for sexual harassment. A duly constituted Internal Complaints Committee (ICC) will address complaints in accordance with the POSH Act, 2013. Awareness sessions on workplace behaviour and grievance redressal will be conducted regularly."},{"n":"7.1","t":"Working Hours","b":"Standard: 9:30 am to 5:30 pm, Monday to Saturday. Flexibility is allowed in field roles as per requirement."},{"n":"7.2","t":"Leave Policy","b":"Casual Leave: 12 days a year. Sick Leave: 10 days a year. Paid Leave: 18 days a year. Maternity Leave: as per statutory norms. Paternity Leave: 7 days. Compensatory Off: for extra work on weekends or public holidays."},{"n":"7.3","t":"Special Leaves","b":"Menstrual Leave: one day a month, on an opt-in basis. Trauma or Emotional Health Leave: up to 5 days a year for staff or leaders with past trauma, on approval."},{"n":"8","t":"Compensation and Benefits","b":"Salaries will be fair, performance-based, and aligned with sector standards. Travel, lodging and food will be reimbursed as per project guidelines. Health insurance is provided for full-time staff. Professional development opportunities are encouraged and supported."},{"n":"9","t":"Performance Evaluation","b":"Conducted twice a year by supervisors or designated managers. Focused on performance, growth and well-being. Constructive feedback, mentoring and capacity-building support are integral to the process."},{"n":"10","t":"Grievance Redressal","b":"Employees can approach the HR Officer, the Grievance Redressal Committee, or the Director depending on the nature of the complaint. Anonymous complaints can be made via a dedicated email ID or suggestion box. All grievances will be resolved within 15 working days. Email: mail@empowerpeople.org.in"},{"n":"11","t":"Exit Policy","b":"Notice period: a minimum of 30 days by either party, unless otherwise agreed. Exit interviews will be conducted to ensure feedback and learning. Final dues and certificates will be cleared within 45 days of exit."},{"n":"12","t":"Child and Vulnerable Adult Protection Policy","b":"All staff and volunteers must adhere to strict guidelines when working with children and vulnerable groups. Any breach will be taken seriously and reported as per POCSO or relevant laws."},{"n":"13","t":"Data Protection and Confidentiality","b":"All information regarding Sakhis, communities, or internal functioning is strictly confidential. Employees must sign a Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) upon joining."},{"n":"14","t":"Amendments and Review","b":"This HR Policy will be reviewed every two years or as needed. Suggestions from employees, Sakhi leaders and field staff are welcome to ensure inclusivity and relevance."}]}];
